Aerial

Two ways the oil and gas industry puts carbon into the air — gas it burns and
gas it leaks — on one map, under one date window.

  make serve     # static server on :8000
  make test      # the reducer's rules, in node
  make vendor    # re-vendor maplibre, duckdb, the dd design system and inter

Three detection layers, all drawn at once, one quarter grid over all of them:

  - S2: Sentinel-2 flare clusters from the data-desk tables in the Data Desk
    CloudFerro archive, read through DuckDB-Wasm.
  - VNF: VIIRS Nightfire, from the eog tables in the same archive, which sees
    flares at night and measures their radiant heat.
  - Methane: dated plume observations from Carbon Mapper, IMEO, SRON, Nature
    Trace and Data Desk. Most carry a measured release rate; Nature Trace
    carries a modeled methane enhancement (ppm·m) and a model confidence, shown
    in place of a rate so it is never read as a mass.

Aerial reads. It publishes nothing and detects nothing of its own: the archive it
draws is built by the etl repo, and the methodology behind the Sentinel-2 tables
is s2-flares (github.com/data-desk-eco/s2-flares).

Shape says whether a feature burned or leaked, and colour says only how much —
flaring on the Data Desk intensity ramp, methane on viridis, because gas
released and gas burned are not one quantity. The two flaring instruments read
on scales that are not comparable either, so the key states each one's units and
its rows are that layer's colour bands: turn a band off to filter it out, turn
them all off to take the layer off the map.

Where things live:

  web/config.js      the whole app declaration, and the orchestration around it
  web/layers.js      marking, ramp and colour policy for every layer
  web/key.js         the key: a group of colour bands per family
  web/shell/         the map shell: mount, panels, key, quarters, card, drawer
  web/card/          one card header, one body per feature kind
  web/nearby.js      the "also here" row across the families
  web/flaring/       the S2 archive and VNF readers, and the feature builders
  web/methane/       plume reader, attribution, candidates, licences, overlay
  web/vendor/        the dd design system, DuckDB, MapLibre, Inter

web/shell/ was the cartograph library until August 2026; the other maps that
vendored it now forward here, so it is first-party code. CLAUDE.md carries the
invariants: read them before changing how a rate is computed.

Zero npm dependencies. MapLibre GL and DuckDB-Wasm are vendored; everything else
is browser built-ins.

References

Faruolo et al. (2024) The DAFI v2 algorithm for gas flare detection
https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/ad82fb
Elvidge et al. (2013) VIIRS Nightfire: satellite pyrometry at night
https://doi.org/10.3390/rs5094423
Jacob et al. (2022) Quantifying methane emissions from the global scale down to
point sources
https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-22-9617-2022
